# How to Run the Application
# Save the code above as deploy_pr.py in your project directory.
# Make sure your .env file is in the same directory.
# Ensure Docker is running.
# Open your terminal and run the script with a GitHub PR link as an argument:
# bash
# python deploy_pr.py "https://github.com/owner/repository-name/pull/123"
# Replace the URL with the actual link to the approved PR you want to deploy.

# Important Considerations
# Dockerfile: This script assumes that the root of the repository contains a valid Dockerfile. Without it, the Docker build step will fail.
# Port Mapping: In the docker_client.containers.run call, I've used ports={'80/tcp': 8080} as an example. This maps port 80 inside the container to port 8080 on your local machine. You must adjust this based on the port that the application inside the Docker container exposes.
# Error Handling: The script includes basic error handling but can be extended for more robust failure management.
# Security: Be mindful that you are running code from a PR on your local machine. Only do this for PRs from repositories you trust.
# This solution provides a solid foundation. You can expand it further by adding a web interface (using Flask or Django), more sophisticated error logging, or options to customize the Docker run command.